the cost of living crisis continues to  bite and this is data from the UK's  largest survey of businesses a British  Chamber of Commerce looking back over  the past quarter now if we take a look  at domestic sales you can see here this  red bar at the bottom this is the number  of businesses who say that their sales  have decreased in the past quarter has  gone up to nearly a quarter as you can  see it's been rising fairly steadily  over the past two years but in  particular if you look up at that blue  bar that's the number of businesses who  show that their sales have increased and  that's dropped quite substantially to  just a third compared to 41 in the last  quarter now if we look ahead to how  businesses are feeling about their  profitability over the next year this is  also a fairly dismal picture so 39 of  businesses now say that they believe  their profits are going to go down next  year compared to around a third who  believe they'll go up and so if you look  at this aggregate data here curve it's  going down it's now going into the  negative and as you can see it's really  nose diving quite dramatically heading  down on its way not as bad as we saw in  the worst of the covid lockdown in 2020  and also of course if you look back to  2008 we've still got some way to go  before we get that serious but obviously  not a good picture about how positive  businesses are feeling there now if we  also take another look at the percentage  of firms who say they're likely to have  to increase their prices over the next  year of course this is a real indicator  of how inflation is going to continue to  increase for some time yet we can see  that although it's not quite as high as  the last quarter it's only notched down  a very little bit so prices set to go up  now important it's important to say that  this survey was done before the  government announced their package of  support for businesses in terms of  energy help and the mini budget of  course but yesterday we had more gloomy  news from the National Grid projecting  the possibility unlikely but a  possibility that we might have to see  three hourly blackouts to deal with  problems with energy Supply which of  course is an absolute blow to the heart  of many businesses Downing Street of  being adamant previously that they're  not going to introduce rationing or tell  people to cut their consumption well  yesterday that's something that Liz  truss wouldn't repeat and there are  calls by many experts for the government  to be doing more to make people use less
 

